1
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:14,000
Now that we've seen what a derivative

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:15,720
means and what it has to do with rates

3
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:17,800
of change, our next step is to learn how

4
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:20,040
to actually compute these guys. As in,

5
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:21,560
if I give you some kind of function with

6
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:23,400
an explicit formula, you'd want to be

7
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:25,120
able to find what the formula for its

8
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:26,680
derivative is.

9
00:00:26,680 --> 00:00:28,280
Maybe it's obvious, but I think it's

10
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:30,520
worth stating explicitly why this is an

11
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:32,920
important thing to be able to do. Why

12
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:34,840
much of a calculus student's time ends

13
00:00:34,840 --> 00:00:36,080
up going towards grappling with

14
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:38,560
derivatives of abstract functions rather

15
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:40,200
than thinking about concrete rate of

16
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:42,200
change problems.

17
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:43,600
It's because a lot of real-world

18
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:45,320
phenomena, the sort of things that we

19
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:47,600
want to use calculus to analyze, are

20
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:50,240
modeled using polynomials, trigonometric

21
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:52,400
functions, exponentials, and other pure

22
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:54,080
functions like that.

23
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:55,640
So, if you build up some fluency with

24
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:57,960
the ideas of rates of change for those

25
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:00,280
kinds of pure abstract functions, it

26
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:01,720
gives you a language to more readily

27
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:03,120
talk about the rates at which things

28
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:05,519
change in concrete situations that you

29
00:01:05,519 --> 00:01:07,920
might be using calculus to model.

30
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:10,200
But, it is way too easy for this process

31
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:12,240
to feel like just memorizing a list of

32
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:14,240
rules. And if that happens, if you get

33
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:16,320
that feeling, it's also easy to lose

34
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:17,760
sight of the fact that derivatives are

35
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:19,640
fundamentally about just looking at tiny

36
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:21,480
changes to some quantity and how that

37
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:23,200
relates to a resulting tiny change in

38
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:24,640
another quantity.

39
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:26,920
So, in this video and in the next one,

40
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:28,440
my aim is to show you how you can think

41
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:30,440
about a few of these rules intuitively

42
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:32,520
and geometrically. And I really want to

43
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:34,560
encourage you to never forget that tiny

44
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:37,960
nudges are at the heart of derivatives.

45
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:39,600
Let's start with a simple function like

46
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:42,000
f of x equals x squared. What if I asked

47
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:43,440
you its derivative?

48
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:44,920
That is, if you were to look at some

49
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:47,840
value x, like x equals 2, and compare it

50
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:50,200
to a value slightly bigger, just dx

51
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:52,520
bigger, what's the corresponding change

52
00:01:52,520 --> 00:01:55,520
in the value of the function, df?

53
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:57,520
And in particular, what's df divided by

54
00:01:57,520 --> 00:01:59,920
dx? The rate at which this function is

55
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:03,160
changing per unit change in x.

56
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:05,160
As a first step for intuition, we know

57
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:07,440
that you can think of this ratio df dx

58
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:09,280
as the slope of a tangent line to the

59
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:11,280
graph of x squared.

60
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:12,680
And from that, you can see that the

61
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:14,640
slope generally increases as x

62
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:17,040
increases. At zero, the tangent line is

63
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:19,000
flat and the slope is zero.

64
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:20,680
At x equals one, it's something a bit

65
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:22,600
steeper.

66
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:25,080
At x equals two, it's steeper still.

67
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:26,880
But looking at graphs isn't generally

68
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:28,840
the best way to understand the precise

69
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:30,680
formula for a derivative.

70
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:32,240
For that, it's best to take a more

71
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:34,320
literal look at what x squared actually

72
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:36,440
means. And in this case, let's go ahead

73
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:38,240
and picture a square whose side length

74
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:40,040
is x.

75
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:42,040
If you increase x by some tiny nudge,

76
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:44,360
some little dx, what's the resulting

77
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:47,720
change in the area of that square?

78
00:02:47,720 --> 00:02:49,959
That slight change in area is what df

79
00:02:49,959 --> 00:02:52,440
means in this context. It's the tiny

80
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:54,720
increase to the value of f of x equals x

81
00:02:54,720 --> 00:02:57,320
squared caused by increasing x by that

82
00:02:57,320 --> 00:02:59,239
tiny nudge dx.

83
00:02:59,239 --> 00:03:00,880
Now, you can see that there's three new

84
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:03,360
bits of area in this diagram. Two thin

85
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:06,120
rectangles and a minuscule square.

86
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:07,880
The two thin rectangles each have side

87
00:03:07,880 --> 00:03:10,360
lengths of x and dx. So, they account

88
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:13,320
for two times x times dx units of new

89
00:03:13,320 --> 00:03:14,519
area.

90
00:03:14,519 --> 00:03:16,320
For example, let's say x was three and

91
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:18,640
dx was 0.01.

92
00:03:18,640 --> 00:03:20,600
Then that new area from these two thin

93
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:22,840
rectangles would be two times three

94
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:26,840
times 0.01, which is 0.06. About six

95
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:29,720
times the size of dx.

96
00:03:29,720 --> 00:03:31,519
That little square there has an area of

97
00:03:31,519 --> 00:03:33,680
dx squared. But you should think of that

98
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:37,600
as being really tiny, negligibly tiny.

99
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:40,760
For example, if dx was 0.01, that would

100
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:43,800
be only 0.0001.

101
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:45,720
And keep in mind, I'm drawing dx with a

102
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:47,360
fair bit of width here just so we can

103
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:49,720
actually see it. But always remember, in

104
00:03:49,720 --> 00:03:51,920
principle, dx should be thought of as a

105
00:03:51,920 --> 00:03:54,000
truly tiny amount.

106
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,280
And for those truly tiny amounts, a good

107
00:03:56,280 --> 00:03:58,200
rule of thumb is that you can ignore

108
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:00,600
anything that includes a DX raised to a

109
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:02,440
power greater than one.

110
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:04,680
That is a tiny change squared is a

111
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:07,440
negligible change.

112
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:09,400
What this leaves us with is that DF is

113
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:11,800
just some multiple of DX.

114
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:13,880
And that multiple, 2X, which you could

115
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:16,680
also write as DF divided by DX, is the

116
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:19,000
derivative of X squared.

117
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:20,760
For example, if you were starting at X

118
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:23,080
equals 3, then as you slightly increase

119
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:25,760
X, the rate of change in the area per

120
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:28,560
unit change in length added, DX squared

121
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:32,560
over DX, would be 2 * 3, or 6.

122
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:34,080
And if instead you were starting at X

123
00:04:34,080 --> 00:04:36,200
equals 5, then the rate of change would

124
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:38,560
be 10 units of area per unit change in

125
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:41,000
X.

126
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:42,680
Let's go ahead and try a different

127
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:45,760
simple function, f of x equals x cubed.

128
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:47,400
This is going to be the geometric view

129
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:48,440
of the stuff that I went through

130
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:50,919
algebraically in the last video.

131
00:04:50,919 --> 00:04:52,280
What's nice here is that we can think of

132
00:04:52,280 --> 00:04:55,200
x cubed as the volume of an actual cube

133
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:57,400
whose side lengths are x.

134
00:04:57,400 --> 00:04:59,440
And when you increase x by a tiny nudge,

135
00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:02,200
a tiny DX, the resulting increase in

136
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:04,960
volume is what I have here in yellow.

137
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:07,120
That represents all the volume in a cube

138
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:09,680
with side lengths x plus DX that's not

139
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:11,560
already in the original cube, the one

140
00:05:11,560 --> 00:05:13,720
with side length x.

141
00:05:13,720 --> 00:05:15,360
It's nice to think of this new volume as

142
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:17,720
broken up into multiple components, but

143
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:20,040
almost all of it comes from these three

144
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:21,360
square faces.

145
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:24,080
Or said a little more precisely, as DX

146
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:26,600
approaches zero, those three squares

147
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:28,840
comprise a portion closer and closer to

148
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:33,760
100% of that new yellow volume.

149
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:35,960
Each of those thin squares has a volume

150
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:39,080
of x squared times DX, the area of the

151
00:05:39,080 --> 00:05:42,200
face times that little thickness DX. So

152
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:45,160
in total, this gives us 3 x squared DX

153
00:05:45,160 --> 00:05:47,200
of volume change.

154
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:49,000
And to be sure, there are other slivers

155
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,160
of volume here along the edges and that

156
00:05:51,160 --> 00:05:53,240
tiny one in the corner, but all of that

157
00:05:53,240 --> 00:05:55,320
volume is going to be proportional to dx

158
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:57,960
squared or dx cubed, so we can safely

159
00:05:57,960 --> 00:05:59,440
ignore them.

160
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:01,040
Again, this is ultimately because

161
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:04,080
they're going to be divided by dx.

162
00:06:04,080 --> 00:06:06,080
And if there's still any dx remaining,

163
00:06:06,080 --> 00:06:07,840
then those terms aren't going to survive

164
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:11,280
the process of letting dx approach zero.

165
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:13,000
What this means is that the derivative

166
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,440
of x cubed, the rate at which x cubed

167
00:06:15,440 --> 00:06:18,800
changes per unit change of x, is 3 * x

168
00:06:18,800 --> 00:06:21,000
squared.

169
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:22,400
What that means in terms of graphical

170
00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:24,880
intuition is that the slope of the graph

171
00:06:24,880 --> 00:06:27,919
of x cubed at every single point x is

172
00:06:27,919 --> 00:06:31,600
exactly 3x squared.

173
00:06:33,919 --> 00:06:35,640
And reasoning about that slope, it

174
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:37,240
should make sense that this derivative

175
00:06:37,240 --> 00:06:39,840
is high on the left and then zero at the

176
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:41,840
origin and then high again as you move

177
00:06:41,840 --> 00:06:44,040
to the right, but just thinking in terms

178
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:45,919
of the graph would never have landed us

179
00:06:45,919 --> 00:06:48,840
on the precise quantity 3x squared.

180
00:06:48,840 --> 00:06:50,200
For that, we had to take a much more

181
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:52,480
direct look at what x cubed actually

182
00:06:52,480 --> 00:06:54,560
means.

183
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:56,000
Now, in practice, you wouldn't

184
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:57,720
necessarily think of the square every

185
00:06:57,720 --> 00:06:59,160
time you're taking the derivative of x

186
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:01,360
squared, nor would you necessarily think

187
00:07:01,360 --> 00:07:02,800
of this cube whenever you're taking the

188
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:05,440
derivative of x cubed. Both of them fall

189
00:07:05,440 --> 00:07:07,280
under a pretty recognizable pattern for

190
00:07:07,280 --> 00:07:09,080
polynomial terms.

191
00:07:09,080 --> 00:07:11,120
The derivative of x to the fourth turns

192
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:13,960
out to be 4x cubed, the derivative of x

193
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:16,960
to the fifth is 5x to the fourth and so

194
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:18,720
on.

195
00:07:18,720 --> 00:07:20,520
Abstractly, you'd write this as the

196
00:07:20,520 --> 00:07:23,680
derivative of x to the n for any power n

197
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:27,520
is n * x to the n - 1.

198
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:29,000
This right here is what's known in the

199
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,640
business as the power rule.

200
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:33,840
In practice, we all quickly just get

201
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:35,919
jaded and think about this symbolically

202
00:07:35,919 --> 00:07:38,000
as the exponent hopping down in front,

203
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,200
leaving behind one less than itself,

204
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:41,720
rarely pausing to think about the

205
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:43,720
geometric delights that underlie these

206
00:07:43,720 --> 00:07:45,200
derivatives.

207
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:46,480
That's the kind of thing that happens

208
00:07:46,480 --> 00:07:47,760
when these tend to fall in the middle of

209
00:07:47,760 --> 00:07:50,480
much longer computations.

210
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:51,920
But rather than chalking it all off to

211
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:53,480
symbolic patterns, let's just take a

212
00:07:53,480 --> 00:07:55,400
moment and think about why this works

213
00:07:55,400 --> 00:07:58,280
for powers beyond just two and three.

214
00:07:58,280 --> 00:08:00,800
When you nudge that input X, increasing

215
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:03,840
it slightly to X + DX, working out the

216
00:08:03,840 --> 00:08:06,200
exact value of that nudged output would

217
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:08,600
involve multiplying together these n

218
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:11,560
separate X + DX terms. The full

219
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:14,120
expansion would be really complicated,

220
00:08:14,120 --> 00:08:16,080
but part of the point of derivatives is

221
00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:17,919
that most of that complication can be

222
00:08:17,919 --> 00:08:19,360
ignored.

223
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:21,560
The first term in your expansion is X to

224
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:24,080
the n. This is analogous to the area of

225
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:25,720
the original square

226
00:08:25,720 --> 00:08:27,720
or the volume of the original cube from

227
00:08:27,720 --> 00:08:30,720
our previous examples.

228
00:08:30,720 --> 00:08:32,680
For the next terms in the expansion, you

229
00:08:32,680 --> 00:08:38,159
can choose mostly X's with a single DX.

230
00:08:41,880 --> 00:08:43,360
Since there are n different

231
00:08:43,360 --> 00:08:44,880
parentheticals from which you could have

232
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:47,760
chosen that single DX, this gives us n

233
00:08:47,760 --> 00:08:50,240
separate terms, all of which include n

234
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:53,360
minus one X's times a DX, giving a value

235
00:08:53,360 --> 00:08:57,400
of X to the power n minus one times DX.

236
00:08:57,400 --> 00:08:59,480
This is analogous to how the majority of

237
00:08:59,480 --> 00:09:01,480
the new area in the square came from

238
00:09:01,480 --> 00:09:04,400
those two bars, each with area X times

239
00:09:04,400 --> 00:09:07,280
DX, or how the bulk of the new volume in

240
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:09,480
the cube came from those three thin

241
00:09:09,480 --> 00:09:11,920
squares, each of which had a volume of X

242
00:09:11,920 --> 00:09:14,720
squared times DX.

243
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:16,440
There will be many other terms in this

244
00:09:16,440 --> 00:09:18,360
expansion, but all of them are just

245
00:09:18,360 --> 00:09:20,640
going to be some multiple of DX squared,

246
00:09:20,640 --> 00:09:22,800
so we can safely ignore them. And what

247
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:25,000
that means is that all but a negligible

248
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,160
portion of the increase in the output

249
00:09:27,160 --> 00:09:29,920
comes from n copies of this X to the n

250
00:09:29,920 --> 00:09:32,920
minus one times DX. That's what it means

251
00:09:32,920 --> 00:09:35,720
for the derivative of X to the n to be n

252
00:09:35,720 --> 00:09:38,920
times X to the n - 1.

253
00:09:38,920 --> 00:09:40,640
And even though, like I said, in

254
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:41,800
practice, you'll find yourself

255
00:09:41,800 --> 00:09:43,520
performing this derivative quickly and

256
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:45,680
symbolically, imagining the exponent

257
00:09:45,680 --> 00:09:47,640
hopping down to the front,

258
00:09:47,640 --> 00:09:49,560
every now and then, it's nice to just

259
00:09:49,560 --> 00:09:51,800
step back and remember why these rules

260
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:52,960
work.

261
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:54,600
Not just because it's pretty, and not

262
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:56,040
just because it helps remind us that

263
00:09:56,040 --> 00:09:58,000
math actually makes sense and isn't just

264
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,160
a pile of formulas to memorize,

265
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:01,800
but because it flexes that very

266
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:03,400
important muscle of thinking about

267
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:07,520
derivatives in terms of tiny nudges.

268
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:08,880
As another example, think of the

269
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:12,640
function f of x = 1 / x.

270
00:10:12,640 --> 00:10:14,440
Now, on the one hand, you could just

271
00:10:14,440 --> 00:10:16,720
blindly try applying the power rule,

272
00:10:16,720 --> 00:10:20,080
since 1 / x is the same as writing x ^

273
00:10:20,080 --> 00:10:21,400
-1.

274
00:10:21,400 --> 00:10:23,320
That would involve letting the -1 hop

275
00:10:23,320 --> 00:10:25,360
down in front, leaving behind one less

276
00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:28,200
than itself, which is -2.

277
00:10:28,200 --> 00:10:29,640
But let's have some fun and see if we

278
00:10:29,640 --> 00:10:31,560
can reason about this geometrically,

279
00:10:31,560 --> 00:10:32,840
rather than just plugging it through

280
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:34,760
some formula.

281
00:10:34,760 --> 00:10:38,200
The value 1 / x is asking, "What number

282
00:10:38,200 --> 00:10:40,920
multiplied by x = 1?"

283
00:10:40,920 --> 00:10:43,280
So, here's how I'd like to visualize it.

284
00:10:43,280 --> 00:10:45,120
Imagine a little rectangular puddle of

285
00:10:45,120 --> 00:10:47,120
water sitting in two dimensions, whose

286
00:10:47,120 --> 00:10:48,880
area is one.

287
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:51,320
And let's say that its width is x, which

288
00:10:51,320 --> 00:10:53,720
means that the height has to be 1 / x,

289
00:10:53,720 --> 00:10:56,360
since the total area of it is one.

290
00:10:56,360 --> 00:10:58,880
So, if x was stretched out to two, then

291
00:10:58,880 --> 00:11:01,800
that height is forced down to 1/2.

292
00:11:01,800 --> 00:11:03,800
And if you increased x up to three, then

293
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:05,280
the other side has to be squished down

294
00:11:05,280 --> 00:11:07,080
to 1/3.

295
00:11:07,080 --> 00:11:08,680
This is a nice way to think about the

296
00:11:08,680 --> 00:11:11,280
graph of 1 / x, by the way.

297
00:11:11,280 --> 00:11:12,960
If you think of this width x of the

298
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:15,520
puddle as being in the xy-plane, then

299
00:11:15,520 --> 00:11:18,520
that corresponding output 1 / x, the

300
00:11:18,520 --> 00:11:21,240
height of the graph above that point, is

301
00:11:21,240 --> 00:11:22,880
whatever the height of your puddle has

302
00:11:22,880 --> 00:11:26,280
to be to maintain an area of one.

303
00:11:26,280 --> 00:11:28,000
So, with this visual in mind, for the

304
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,280
derivative, imagine nudging up that

305
00:11:30,280 --> 00:11:32,640
value of x by some tiny amount, some

306
00:11:32,640 --> 00:11:34,560
tiny dx.

307
00:11:34,560 --> 00:11:36,520
How must the height of this rectangle

308
00:11:36,520 --> 00:11:38,840
change so that the area of the puddle

309
00:11:38,840 --> 00:11:41,160
remains constant at one?

310
00:11:41,160 --> 00:11:44,080
That is, increasing the width by dx adds

311
00:11:44,080 --> 00:11:46,480
some new area to the right here. So, the

312
00:11:46,480 --> 00:11:48,920
puddle has to decrease in height by some

313
00:11:48,920 --> 00:11:52,320
d 1/x so that the area lost off of that

314
00:11:52,320 --> 00:11:56,000
top cancels out the area gained.

315
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,160
You should think of that d 1/x as being

316
00:11:58,160 --> 00:12:00,160
a negative amount, by the way, since

317
00:12:00,160 --> 00:12:01,720
it's decreasing the height of the

318
00:12:01,720 --> 00:12:03,760
rectangle.

319
00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:05,360
And you know what? I'm going to leave

320
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:07,320
the last few steps here for you, for you

321
00:12:07,320 --> 00:12:08,800
to pause and ponder and work out an

322
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:10,440
ultimate expression.

323
00:12:10,440 --> 00:12:12,880
And once you reason out what d of 1/x

324
00:12:12,880 --> 00:12:15,200
divided by dx should be, I want you to

325
00:12:15,200 --> 00:12:16,760
compare it to what you would have gotten

326
00:12:16,760 --> 00:12:18,280
if you had just blindly applied the

327
00:12:18,280 --> 00:12:21,000
power rule purely symbolically to x to

328
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,840
the negative one.

329
00:12:24,160 --> 00:12:25,600
And while I'm encouraging you to pause

330
00:12:25,600 --> 00:12:27,280
and ponder, here's another fun challenge

331
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:29,040
if you're feeling up to it.

332
00:12:29,040 --> 00:12:30,560
See if you can reason through what the

333
00:12:30,560 --> 00:12:32,440
derivative of the square root of x

334
00:12:32,440 --> 00:12:35,080
should be.

335
00:12:36,680 --> 00:12:38,320
To finish things off, I want to tackle

336
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:40,520
one more type of function, trigonometric

337
00:12:40,520 --> 00:12:42,680
functions. And in particular, let's

338
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:45,200
focus on the sine function.

339
00:12:45,200 --> 00:12:46,440
So, for this section, I'm going to

340
00:12:46,440 --> 00:12:48,080
assume that you're already familiar with

341
00:12:48,080 --> 00:12:50,280
how to think about trig functions using

342
00:12:50,280 --> 00:12:52,160
the unit circle, the circle with the

343
00:12:52,160 --> 00:12:55,160
radius one centered at the origin.

344
00:12:55,160 --> 00:12:57,200
For a given value of theta, like say

345
00:12:57,200 --> 00:13:00,040
0.8, you imagine yourself walking around

346
00:13:00,040 --> 00:13:01,800
the circle starting from the rightmost

347
00:13:01,800 --> 00:13:03,920
point until you've traversed that

348
00:13:03,920 --> 00:13:07,080
distance of 0.8 in arc length.

349
00:13:07,080 --> 00:13:08,480
This is the same thing as saying that

350
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:11,000
the angle right here is exactly theta

351
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,400
radians since the circle has a radius of

352
00:13:13,400 --> 00:13:14,720
one.

353
00:13:14,720 --> 00:13:17,240
Then, what sine of theta means is the

354
00:13:17,240 --> 00:13:20,880
height of that point above the x-axis.

355
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:23,040
And as your theta value increases and

356
00:13:23,040 --> 00:13:25,160
you walk around the circle, your height

357
00:13:25,160 --> 00:13:29,040
bobs up and down between -1 and 1.

358
00:13:29,040 --> 00:13:31,240
So, when you graph sin of theta versus

359
00:13:31,240 --> 00:13:34,000
theta, you get this wave pattern, the

360
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:37,640
quintessential wave pattern.

361
00:13:37,640 --> 00:13:39,600
And just from looking at this graph, we

362
00:13:39,600 --> 00:13:41,520
can start to get a feel for the shape of

363
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:43,920
the derivative of the sine.

364
00:13:43,920 --> 00:13:46,440
The slope at zero is something positive,

365
00:13:46,440 --> 00:13:49,560
since sin of theta is increasing there.

366
00:13:49,560 --> 00:13:51,080
And as we move to the right and sin of

367
00:13:51,080 --> 00:13:53,160
